anian Empire"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":"Roman Empire","explanation":"The Byzantine Empire developed from the eastern Roman Empire; 'Byzantine' is a later historical label.","format":"distinction","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Byzantine","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/byzantium-ca-330-1453"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"editorial-v76","editorialStatus74":"ready","skill74":"distinction","topic74":"Byzantine","difficulty74":"unrated","lineage74":"Original editorial item; Alpha transcription used to benchmark concise options. Not an official CISIA question.","evidence":{"type":"editorial","referenceBasis":"Institutional reference","checkedAt":"2026-10-05"},"editorial75":{"sourceKey":"byzantium","optionWords":[2,2,2,2,2],"difficultyStatus":"Not calibrated with candidates","syllabusArea":"historical-cultural-space-time"},"fingerprint":"8fe44d41dd54e04b6b52"},{"id":"GEN43-Q01008","qid":1008,"nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-008","relatedNodeIds":["EDITORIAL76-008"],"factIds":["EDITORIAL76-008"],"major":"Art History","group":"Antonello","stem":"In Antonello’s Palermo Virgin Annunciate, which participant is outside the painted scene?","choices":["Saint Anne","Joseph","John the Baptist","Gabriel","Elizabeth"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":"Gabriel","explanation":"The Annunciation is evoked without showing Gabriel; Mary addresses an implied presence beyond the panel.","format":"iconography","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Antonello","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www2.regione.sicilia.it/beniculturali/dirbenicult/info/beniinamovibili/PaAnnunziata.html"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"editorial-v76","editorialStatus74":"ready","skill74":"iconography","topic74":"Antonello","difficulty74":"unrated","lineage74":"Original editorial item; Alpha transcription used to benchmark concise options. Not an official CISIA question.","evidence":{"type":"editorial","referenceBasis":"Institutional reference","checkedAt":"2026-10-05"},"editorial75":{"sourceKey":"annunciata76","optionWords":[2,1,3,1,1],"difficultyStatus":"Not calibrated with candidates","syllabusArea":"historical-cultural-space-time"},"fingerprint":"4a9a65795e3cc44ee955","imageId":"T81-t63-4b6ed32f2501","imageUse81":"The identified work illustrates the feature or context tested; metadata revealed only after answering."},{"id":"GEN43-Q01009","qid":1009,"nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-009","relatedNodeIds":["EDITORIAL74-009"],"factIds":["EDITORIAL74-009"],"major":"Art History","group":"Cubism","stem":"Which material gives papier collé its name?","choices":["Pasted paper","Woven silk","Etched copper","Coloured wax","Incised plaster"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":"Pasted paper","explanation":"Papier collé uses pasted paper, a technique associated with Synthetic Cubist experiments.","format":"technique","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Cubism","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/cubism"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"editorial-v76","editorialStatus74":"ready","skill74":"technique","topic74":"Cubism","difficulty74":"unrated","lineage74":"Original editorial item; Alpha transcription used to benchmark concise options. Not an official CISIA question.","evidence":{"type":"editorial","referenceBasis":"Institutional reference","checkedAt":"2026-10-05"},"editorial75":{"sourceKey":"cubism","optionWords":[2,2,2,2,2],"difficultyStatus":"Not calibrated with candidates","syllabusArea":"historical-cultural-space-time"},"fingerprint":"314de94d3fefaa1ddf7e"},{"id":"GEN43-Q01010","qid":1010,"nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-010","relatedNodeIds":["EDITORIAL74-010"],"factIds":["EDITORIAL74-010"],"major":"Art History","group":"Cubism","stem":"Which practice is least consistent with Cubism?","choices":["Collage","Single-point illusionism","Faceted form","Fragmented objects","Multiple viewpoints"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":"Single-point illusionism","explanation":"Cubist experiments challenged unified Renaissance perspective; collage and fragmentation were important methods.","format":"distinction","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Cubism","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/cubism"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"editorial-v76","editorialStatus74":"ready","skill74":"distinction","topic74":"Cubism","difficulty74":"unrated","lineage74":"Original editorial item; Alpha transcription used to benchmark concise options. Not an official CISIA question.","evidence":{"type":"editorial","referenceBasis":"Institutional reference","checkedAt":"2026-10-05"},"editorial75":{"sourceKey":"cubism","optionWords":[1,2,2,2,2],"difficultyStatus":"Not calibrated with candidates","syllabusArea":"historical-cultural-space-time"},"fingerprint":"7d6aafe6c302da08b9e7"},{"id":"GEN43-Q01011","qid":1011,"nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-011","relatedNodeIds":["EDITORIAL74-011"],"factIds":["EDITORIAL74-011"],"major":"Art History","group":"Impressionism","stem":"The Impressionists’ first independent exhibition in 1874 offered an alternative to which institution?","choices":["Bauhaus","Paris Salon","Salon d’Automne","Vienna Secession","Salon des Indépendants"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":"Paris Salon","explanation":"The official Paris Salon dominated exhibition opportunities; the other organizations were founded later.","format":"context","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Impressionism","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/impressionism-art-and-modernity"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"editorial-v76","editorialStatus74":"ready","skill74":"context","topic74":"Impressionism","difficulty74":"unrated","lineage74":"Original editorial item; Alpha transcription used to benchmark concise options. Not an official CISIA question.","evidence":{"type":"editorial","referenceBasis":"Institutional reference","checkedAt":"2026-10-05"},"editorial75":{"sourceKey":"impressionism","optionWords":[1,2,2,2,3],"difficultyStatus":"Not calibrated with candidates","syllabusArea":"historical-cultural-space-time"},"fingerprint":"cfedfc53c07135d6d4cd"},{"id":"GEN43-Q01012","qid":1012,"nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-012","relatedNodeIds":["EDITORIAL74-012"],"factIds":["EDITORIAL74-012"],"major":"Art History","group":"Impressionism","stem":"Monet’s repeated views of one subject primarily explore changes in what?","choices":["Patronage","Light","Anatomy","Iconography","Proportion"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":"Light","explanation":"Impressionist practice often emphasizes light, atmosphere and perception rather than a subject's fixed outline.","format":"interpretation","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Impressionism","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/impressionism-art-and-modernity"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"editorial-v76","editorialStatus74":"ready","skill74":"interpretation","topic74":"Impressionism","difficulty74":"unrated","lineage74":"Original editorial item; Alpha transcription used to benchmark concise options. Not an official CISIA question.","evidence":{"type":"editorial","referenceBasis":"Institutional reference","checkedAt":"2026-10-05"},"editorial75":{"sourceKey":"impressionism","optionWords":[1,1,1,1,1],"difficultyStatus":"Not calibrated with candidates","syllabusArea":"historical-cultural-space-time"},"fingerprint":"9c92a4c50bd4d2d50e32"},{"id":"GEN43-Q01013","qid":1013,"nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-013","relatedNodeIds":["EDITORIAL74-013"],"factIds":["EDITORIAL74-013"],"major":"Art History","group":"Impressionism","stem":"Which feature is least characteristic of Impressionism?","choices":["Modern leisure","Changing light","Outdoor painting","Broken brushwork","Academic contour"],"correctIndex":4,"answer":"Academic contour","explanation":"Impressionist brushwork often loosens the academic emphasis on sharply bounded forms.","format":"negative","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Impressionism","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/impressionism-art-and-modernity"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"editorial-v76","editorialStatus74":"ready","skill74":"negative","topic74":"Impressionism","difficulty74":"unrated","lineage74":"Original editorial item; Alpha transcription used to benchmark concise options. Not an official CISIA question.","evidence":{"type":"editorial","referenceBasis":"Institutional reference","checkedAt":"2026-10-05"},"editorial75":{"sourceKey":"impressionism","optionWords":[2,2,2,2,2],"difficultyStatus":"Not calibrated with candidates","syllabusArea":"historical-cultural-space-time"},"fingerprint":"ec6fc68dcf2ba71c25a1"},{"id":"GEN43-Q01014","qid":1014,"nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-014","relatedNodeIds":["EDITORIAL74-014"],"factIds":["EDITORIAL74-014"],"major":"Art History","group":"Fauvism","stem":"A portrait uses intense green and violet on a face without trying to imitate its natural skin colour. Which movement most directly supports this use of colour?","choices":["Academic Realism","Naturalism","Fauvism","Neo-Impressionism","Neoclassicism"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":"Fauvism","explanation":"Fauvist colour could function expressively and independently of naturalistic description.","format":"distinction","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Fauvism","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/fauvism"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"editorial-v76","editorialStatus74":"ready","skill74":"distinction","topic74":"Fauvism","difficulty74":"unrated","lineage74":"Original editorial item; Alpha transcription used to benchmark concise options. Not an official CISIA question.","evidence":{"type":"editorial","referenceBasis":"Institutional reference","checkedAt":"2026-10-05"},"editorial75":{"sourceKey":"fauvism","optionWords":[2,1,1,1,1],"difficultyStatus":"Not calibrated with candidates","syllabusArea":"historical-cultural-space-time"},"fingerprint":"1ddba3ece5c1ea36dcad"},{"id":"GEN43-Q01015","qid":1015,"nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-015","relatedNodeIds":["EDITORIAL74-015"],"factIds":["EDITORIAL74-015"],"major":"Art History","group":"Surrealism","stem":"Which intellectual field was especially influential on Surrealist interest in dreams and the unconscious?","choices":["Empirical optics","Positivist sociology","Psychoanalysis","Classical rhetoric","Utilitarian ethics"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":"Psychoanalysis","explanation":"Surrealists drew on psychoanalytic ideas about dreams and unconscious mental processes.","format":"context","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Surrealism","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/surrealism"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"editorial-v76","editorialStatus74":"ready","skill74":"context","topic74":"Surrealism","difficulty74":"unrated","lineage74":"Original editorial item; Alpha transcription used to benchmark concise options. Not an official CISIA question.","evidence":{"type":"editorial","referenceBasis":"Institutional reference","checkedAt":"2026-10-05"},"editorial75":{"sourceKey":"surrealism","optionWords":[2,2,1,2,2],"difficultyStatus":"Not calibrated with candidates","syllabusArea":"historical-cultural-space-time"},"fingerprint":"881990305d419a1c7f0e"},{"id":"GEN43-Q01016","qid":1016,"nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-016","relatedNodeIds":["EDITORIAL74-016"],"factIds":["EDITORIAL74-016"],"major":"Art History","group":"Surrealism","stem":"Surrealist automatism primarily sought to suspend what?","choices":["Unconscious impulses","Spontaneous association","Chance effects","Conscious control","Free invention"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":"Conscious control","explanation":"Automatism aimed to allow associations and marks to emerge with reduced conscious direction.","format":"technique","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Surrealism","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/surrealism"}],"examLan